==========================================================================
THE (k = 4) HONEST COLUMN AS A FUNCTION OF LEMMA B's CONSTANT
==========================================================================

PART 0.  GLOBAL, NOT PATCHED: a line-by-line scaling audit.
  The violation occurs from (k = 3, n = 4) upward at perturbations as
  small as t = 1/60, so the slice constant is globally unavailable and
  the re-run must parametrise EVERY line that uses it, not two cells.
  Audit: recompute every line at c and at 2c and report the ratio.
  1.000 = independent of the constant, 1.414 = square root, 2.000 =
  linear.  The c-dependent set must be exactly {core4, y3q, y3l}.

   line   |  ratio at n=10 |  ratio at n=16 | reading
   core3  |       1.000000 |       1.000000 | independent
   core4  |       2.000000 |       2.000000 | linear
   xinv   |       1.000000 |       1.000000 | independent
   y3q    |       1.414214 |       1.414214 | sqrt
   tail   |       1.000000 |       1.000000 | independent
   x1     |       1.000000 |       1.000000 | independent
   y1     |       1.000000 |       1.000000 | independent
   y2     |       1.000000 |       1.000000 | independent
   y3l    |       1.414214 |       1.414214 | sqrt

  c-dependent lines: ['core4', 'y3l', 'y3q']   expected ['core4', 'y3l', 'y3q']   match True

  TWO FACTS I DO NOT INHERIT, checked rather than assumed:
  (i) the xinv line contains a factor sqrt(1 - 1/n) that LOOKS like the
      slice constant but is not: it comes from V <= 2Q(M - Q/n) with
      M <= Q, and M <= Q is unconditional since M = max_i r_i <=
      sum_i r_i = Q.  The audit above confirms xinv does not scale
      with c, so it is correctly left fixed.
  (ii) the ENTRY bound is a separate fact and I never use its slice
      form.  My m = 3 line uses the PER-ENTRY bound
      z_ij >= -(1/n + x_i + y_j), which follows from A >= 0 alone, and
      carries the Xinv correction through the merge with total
      coefficient (3n-4)/3.  So the entry-bound question, refuted or
      not, does not touch this column.

